{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אלה תולדות נח", :translit "eleh toldot noach", :gloss "These are the generations of Noah"} {:he "ד' דסמיכי אלה תולדות", :translit "da' dismichei eleh toldot", :gloss "Four of juxtaposed 'eleh toldot'"} {:he "אלה תולדות השמים", :translit "eleh toldot ha-shamayim", :gloss "These are the generations of the heavens"} {:he "אלה תולדות נח", :translit "eleh toldot noach", :gloss "These are the generations of Noah"} {:he "אלה תולדות שם", :translit "eleh toldot shem", :gloss "These are the generations of Shem"} {:he "אלה תולדות יעקב", :translit "eleh toldot ya'akov", :gloss "These are the generations of Jacob"} {:he "פסל", :translit "pasal", :gloss "invalid / disqualified"} {:he "תהו ובהו", :translit "tohu va-vohu", :gloss "unformed and void"} {:he "דורות שלפניו", :translit "dorot she-lefanav", :gloss "generations before him"} {:he "בני חם ובני יפת", :translit "bnei cham u-vnei yefet", :gloss "children of Ham and children of Japheth"} {:he "עשו ואלופיו", :translit "esav ve-alufav", :gloss "Esau and his chieftains"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 4, :occurrences ["אלה תולדות השמים" "אלה תולדות נח" "אלה תולדות שם" "אלה תולדות יעקב"]} {:kind :other, :note "Pattern observation: in every instance of 'eleh toldot,' what precedes is disqualified (pasal)"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'eleh toldot' marks a genealogical new beginning, and the tradition reads each of these four occurrences as a boundary: what came before is set aside as invalid. Genesis 1:1–2 places tohu va-vohu before 'the generations of the heavens' (Genesis 2:4). The generations before Noah perished in the flood (Genesis 6–7). After Shem, the lines of Ham and Japheth are displaced (Genesis 10). After Jacob, Esau's line of chieftains is set aside (Genesis 36).", :sources ["Genesis 2:4" "Genesis 6:9" "Genesis 10:1" "Genesis 36:1" "Genesis 36:40"]}} {:quotes [{:he "נח נח וגו'", :translit "noach noach ve-gomer", :gloss "Noah Noah etc."} {:he "ג' פעמים כתיב נח בפסוק", :translit "glish pa'amim katuv noach ba-pasuk", :gloss "three times is written Noah in the verse"} {:he "שראה ג' עולמות", :translit "she-ra'ah glish olamot", :gloss "that he saw three worlds"} {:he "שהיה משלשה", :translit "she-hayah meshalesheh", :gloss "that he was of three"} {:he "שכל אחד הציל ג' בזכותו", :translit "she-kol echad hitzil glish bi-zchuto", :gloss "that each saved three by his merit"} {:he "נח הציל ג' בניו שהם שם וחם ויפת", :translit "noach hitzil glish bnav she-hem shem ve-cham ve-yefet", :gloss "Noah saved his three sons, who are Shem, Ham, and Japheth"} {:he "דניאל הציל חנניה מישאל ועזריה בחלום", :translit "daniel hitzil chananyah mishael va-azaryah ba-chalom", :gloss "Daniel saved Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah through a dream"} {:he "איוב הציל שלשה רעיו אליפז התימני ובלדד השוחי וצופר הנעמתי", :translit "iyov hitzil sheloshah re'av elifaz ha-teimani ve-bildad ha-shuchi ve-tzofar ha-na'amati", :gloss "Job saved his three friends, Eliphaz the Temanite, Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite"} {:he "נח לשמים נח לבריות", :translit "noach la-shamayim noach la-briyot", :gloss "Noah to the heavens, Noah to the creatures"} {:he "נח לעליונים נח לתחתונים", :translit "noach le-elyonim noach le-tachtonim", :gloss "Noah to those above, Noah to those below"} {:he "נח בעולם הזה נח בעולם הבא", :translit "noach ba-olam ha-zeh noach ba-olam ha-ba", :gloss "Noah in this world, Noah in the world to come"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["נח נח וגו'"]} {:kind :other, :note "Three-fold pattern: Noah, Daniel, and Job each saved three people by merit"} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay on name נח (rest) as 'rest' to heavens, creatures, above, below, this world, world to come"}], :frame {:text "Noah's name is written three times in Genesis 6:9–10 (נח איש צדיק … נח נח … ויולד נח), and the verse calls him one who will bring rest (Genesis 5:29). Daniel interpreted dreams that saved his three companions (Daniel 2:48–49 in relation to Daniel 3). Job's three friends are named in Job 2:11.", :sources ["Genesis 5:29" "Genesis 6:9" "Daniel 2:48" "Daniel 3:12" "Job 2:11"]}} {:quotes [{:he "תמים היה", :translit "tamim hayah", :gloss "wholehearted he was"} {:he "הי\"ה עולה למנין כ'", :translit "heh-yud-heh oleh le-mispar kaf", :gloss "hei-yud-hei rises to the count of 20"} {:he "שהיה תמים בכל כ' דורות שמאדם עד אברהם", :translit "she-hayah tamim be-chol kaf dorot me-adam ad avraham", :gloss "that he was wholehearted throughout all 20 generations from Adam to Abraham"} {:he "אבל משבא אברהם לא נחשב תמים", :translit "aval mi-she-ba avraham lo necheshav tamim", :gloss "but once Abraham came, he was not counted as wholehearted"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "הי\"ה", :b "כ'"}], :frame {:text "Genesis 6:9 says Noah was 'tamim' (wholehearted). Abraham is told 'walk before me and be tamim' (Genesis 17:1). The midrash contrasts Noah with Abraham: Noah is called wholehearted only relative to his generation, while Abraham walked before God on his own (Genesis Rabbah 30:10). The count of twenty generations from Adam to Abraham is a standard reckoning: ten from Adam to Noah (Genesis 5) and ten from Shem to Abraham (Genesis 11).", :sources ["Genesis 6:9" "Genesis 17:1" "Genesis 5" "Genesis 11" "Genesis Rabbah 30:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "את האלהים התהלך נח", :translit "et ha-elohim hit'halech noach", :gloss "with God walked Noah"} {:he "ס\"ת חכם", :translit "sat chacham", :gloss "end-letters samekh-tav of [the form] chacham"} {:he "ולוקח נפשות חכם", :translit "ve-lokeach nafshot chacham", :gloss "and takes souls — wise"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "את האלהים התהלך נח", :spells "סת"}], :frame {:text "Genesis 6:9 says Noah 'walked with God.' The hidden word סת formed by the end-letters alludes to Proverbs 11:30, 'he who takes souls is wise' (ולוקח נפשות חכם), in which the final letters of the last two words spell סת. The Talmud applies this verse to the righteous who win souls for Torah and good deeds (Sanhedrin 99a).", :sources ["Genesis 6:9" "Proverbs 11:30" "Sanhedrin 99a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 4, :occurrences ["אלה תולדות השמים" "אלה תולדות נח" "אלה תולדות שם" "אלה תולדות יעקב"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "אלה תולדות השמים", :refs ["Genesis 2:4"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "אלה תולדות נח", :refs ["Genesis 6:9"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "אלה תולדות שם", :refs ["Genesis 11:10"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "אלה תולדות יעקב", :refs ["Genesis 37:2"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "Pattern observation: in every instance of 'eleh toldot,' what precedes is disqualified (pasal)", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["נח נח וגו'"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "נח נח וגו'", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "Three-fold pattern: Noah, Daniel, and Job each saved three people by merit", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay on name נח (rest) as 'rest' to heavens, creatures, above, below, this world, world to come", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "הי\"ה", :a-value 20, :b "כ'", :b-value 20, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "את האלהים התהלך נח", :spells "סת", :letters ["ת" "ם" "ך" "ח"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:05:23.473271290Z", :model "glm-5.2"}